mk("xlBInB7BOzE","The VITAL Drum Sound Design Tutorial!! (FREE PLUGIN)",
 "synth-drum-kit-from-scratch-808-sine-45hz-saw-noise-transient-gentle-20hz-hp-narrow-78hz-dip-kick-fast-pitch-env-clap-three-noise-bursts-1k-6k-snare-octave-sines-noise-hats-test-in-context",
 "Synthesize a whole drum kit (808, kick, clap, snare, closed and open hat) from a basic synth: sine and noise sources, envelope-drawn transients, fast pitch envelopes, and targeted EQ, then test and re-tweak each sound inside a real beat",
 "Build six drum sounds in one synth with no samples. 808: sine tuned so its fundamental sits around 45 Hz (an octave below where it first lands), plus a saw and white noise whose levels follow a drawn envelope with a quick dip for the transient; low-pass the saw; boost mids a little, soft-clip, use a very gentle high-pass around 20 Hz (a steep one sounds bad), and a narrow bell cut around 78 Hz; optional parallel copy with reverb faded in and kept to the sides. Kick: sine pitched down, very short amp envelope, a very fast pitch envelope (about 0.07 s) on pitch, soft clip, light bit-crush, multiband drive, cut boxy mids around 400 Hz, high-pass. Clap: white noise whose level envelope has three quick transients then one longer decay (about 0.085 s spacing on a 9-step grid), band-limited (energy above about 600 Hz, key areas about 1 kHz and 6 kHz), high end added with bells not shelves, a little chorus and very short delays for width, compression, reduce side highs. Snare: two or three sines in octaves (the reference showed tones at about 240, 484 and 1 kHz), noise with two quick transients, one shared filter, a very short pitch envelope on all tones, distortion; optionally layer the clap. Closed hat: white noise, about 0.22 s envelope, band/high-pass that keeps some mids. Open hat: longer noise envelope with a second transient, low-cut reverb, very short (1/64) ping-pong delay, shorten decay to taste. Render each and tweak inside a beat (e.g. re-export pitched up a few semitones).",
 ["A synth with sine/saw oscillators, white noise, drawable envelopes or multi-stage LFOs in envelope mode, two filters and basic FX","A spectrum analyser","A DAW to render and test the sounds in a beat"],
 [("tempo_bpm","number","Tempo of the test beat",False),("kit_targets","list","Which of 808, kick, clap, snare, closed hat, open hat to build",True)],
 [("drum_samples","audio","Rendered one-shots for each kit piece"),("drum_patches","synth-patch","Editable synth patches for each piece"),("test_beat","midi","A short beat used to audition and refine the kit")],
 ["Third-party plug-ins named in the video (mastering suite, saturator, multiband distortion) are replaced by generic EQ, soft clip, multiband drive and limiter.",
  "The 78 Hz narrow cut on the 808 is the speaker's ear-matched trick from a reference; tune the notch to taste for your 808 pitch.",
  "Hats and claps are judged in context: a sound that seems fine solo can be too low or too bright in a loop.",
  "For Polyrhythm Studio/Web Audio: oscillators + noise buffer + gain envelopes + biquad filters reproduce every recipe here.",A],
 [("808: sine osc tuned so the fundamental reads about 45 Hz on an analyser; add a saw and white noise whose levels follow a drawn envelope (quick dip at the start for a transient, then decay, about 1.6 s overall); low-pass the saw.","Synth + analyser","A deep sine 808 with a short gritty attack."),
  ("808 processing: small mid boost, soft distortion with modest drive, very gentle high-pass near 20 Hz (not steep), narrow bell cut around 78 Hz; optional parallel copy with reverb faded in, kept to the sides, then a limiter on the pair.","EQ, soft clip, reverb, limiter","Full low end without boom smear; the steep-HP version sounds clearly worse."),
  ("Kick: sine pitched down an octave, very short amp envelope, a very fast envelope (about 0.07 s) routed to pitch, soft clip; then light bit-crush, multiband drive, a cut around 400 Hz for boxiness and a high-pass.","Synth + FX","A short punchy kick with a pitch-drop click."),
  ("Clap: white noise with a level envelope of three quick transients then one longer decay (grid of about 9 steps, about 0.085 s); band-pass it so energy sits above about 600 Hz with emphasis near 1 kHz and 6 kHz; add highs with bells not shelves and avoid much content above about 11 kHz.","Synth + EQ","A recognisable clap 'flam' with no harsh fizz."),
  ("Clap finishing: a little chorus and very short delays for stereo, compression, then turn down side highs.","Chorus, delay, compressor, M/S EQ","Slightly wide, controlled clap."),
  ("Snare: two or three sines an octave apart (reference tones about 240 Hz, 484 Hz, 1 kHz), white noise with two quick transients, all through one filter; route a very short envelope to the pitch of every tone; add distortion; optionally layer the clap and blend.","Synth","A tonal snappy snare distinct from the clap."),
  ("Closed hat: white noise with a very short envelope (about 0.22 s), filter to a tick, high-pass but keep some mids. Open hat: same noise, longer envelope with a second small transient, low-cut reverb and a 1/64 ping-pong delay, then shorten the decay.","Synth + reverb + delay","Closed tick and open wash from one source."),
  ("Render all pieces, build a short beat, and adjust each sound in context (EQ, decay, pitch shift a few semitones and re-export) until they sit together.","DAW","A coherent kit validated in a real groove.")],
 ["Analyser shows the 808 fundamental near 45 Hz and no steep high-pass slope.","The clap waveform shows three discrete bursts before the decay.","Snare shows octave-related tonal peaks plus noise; hats have little energy below a few kHz but are not purely top-end.","Every piece was auditioned and adjusted in a beat, not only solo."],
 ["drums","drum-synthesis","808","kick","clap","snare","hi-hat","punch","web-audio"],A)

mk("_cVKvtFm8J0","How To Synth 5: Synthetic Ambient Orchestraish Strings",
 "hidden-context-synth-strings-two-opposite-direction-arpeggiated-saw-plucks-brassy-filter-env-vibrato-spectral-phaser-above-fundamental-shared-pitch-mod-shimmer-reverb-gated-glimpses-under-sidechain",
 "Make synth layers read as strings or orchestra by obscuring them: two saw-based arpeggiated plucks moving in opposite directions, a fast-open filter envelope and vibrato, a shared pitch-shifting modulated reverb, and level automation plus sidechain so the listener only hears brief glimpses without context",
 "Imply acoustic strings with simple synthesis plus psychoacoustics. Harmonic content: saw-like spectra (strings and brass are close to a saw series). Layer two arpeggiators playing the same chords: one rising, one reversed so they cross; double the arp step time (half speed); each voice uses a brass-like filter envelope (opens fast, decays: a smoothed pluck), light unison, real vibrato, and a spectral/frequency-mode phaser masked to act above the fundamental so the note pitch stays clean; the second voice has a longer pluck, darker filter and more unison. Arp cycle length depends on chord note count, so voice chords deliberately. Send both to one shared reverb with large size and diffusion, low feedback, a downward pitch shimmer and high modulation depth. Then automate the layer's level by feel so it only comes through at chosen moments, and sidechain it under the drums; a supporting soft chord stack gets a big reverb with a gate so tails don't run on. The brain fills in an orchestra because it never hears the sound's before/after context.",
 ["A synth with saw/additive oscillators, filter envelope, vibrato, unison and an arpeggiator","A reverb with pitch-shift (shimmer) and modulation, or a reverb plus pitch shifter","Volume automation and a sidechain ducker"],
 [("chords","midi","Chord progression to arpeggiate (note count sets arp cycle length)",True),("drum_bus","audio","Source for sidechain ducking",False)],
 [("string_layer","audio","Two crossing arps through shared shimmer reverb, gated by automation and sidechain"),("patches","synth-patch","Two arp pluck voices")],
 ["The video uses an additive synth's spectral phaser and a commercial shimmer reverb; map to: phaser or slow harmonic-pitch modulation above the fundamental, and any reverb with pitch-shifted feedback.",
  "Matt prefers steady melodies: keep vibrato and reverb pitch modulation moderate, and rely on obscuring (level automation, sidechain, density) more than wobble.",
  "This is not physical modelling; it approximates a string-section impression via harmonics, space and masking.",A],
 [("Program the chords with a fixed note count (e.g. 3 notes) so arp cycles stay predictable; arp cycle length grows with note count.","Piano roll","Predictable arp phrase length."),
  ("Voice 1: saw-like oscillator, light unison, vibrato on, filter envelope that opens fast then decays (brass-style smoothed pluck), arpeggiator going up at half speed (double the default step time).","Synth + arp","A soft rising arp pluck."),
  ("Add a phaser (or harmonic pitch modulation) masked so it only affects partials above the fundamental, so pitch stays clean while upper harmonics shimmer.","Phaser / spectral mod","Movement in the overtones, steady note pitch."),
  ("Voice 2: same idea with a longer pluck decay, lower filter, a bit more unison, arp direction reversed (starts at the top) so the two layers cross.","Second synth + arp","Two interleaved arps moving in opposite directions."),
  ("Send both voices to one shared reverb: large size and diffusion, low feedback, pitch shift down in the feedback, high modulation depth; high-pass and lift the top before the reverb.","Shimmer reverb","A busy, ensemble-like wash."),
  ("Automate the layer's level by feel so it only comes through at selected moments, and sidechain it under the drums.","Volume automation + sidechain","Brief glimpses that read as strings in the mix, not as a synth."),
  ("Optional support: a soft sustained chord stack with big reverb, gated so tails stop when the chord stops, with a fader dip so it starts large and smooths out.","Synth + reverb + gate","A semi-orchestral bed without long smears.")],
 ["Solo, the layer sounds clearly synthetic; in the full mix with automation and sidechain it reads as strings.","The two arps move in opposite directions and share one reverb return.","Note pitch stays stable; modulation is audible mainly in the upper harmonics and reverb."],
 ["strings","orchestral","arpeggiator","shimmer-reverb","psychoacoustics","layering","sidechain","cinematic"],A)

mk("H6i55lXAMh0","Bass Sound Design: 808s, Plucks, Growls and Sub Bass",
 "four-punchy-bass-archetypes-808-sine-hold-decay-35ms-pitch-env-punch-tube-drive-100hz-lift-attack-delay-vs-kick-pluck-mono-square-lp24-env-plus-wide-pwm-unison-saw-growl-lfo-duck-sub-sine-5ms-attack",
 "Design four bass types from scratch with fit-to-kick rules: a punchy 808 (sine, hold and 1.5 s decay, a 35 ms pitch-envelope punch, tube drive, low boost, attack delayed 80-100 ms when the kick already punches), a pluck bass (mono square through an enveloped 24 dB low-pass plus a wide unison top layer), a sidechained saw growl, and a click-free sine sub",
 "Four punchy bass recipes. 808: one sine osc, attack near 0 (raise slightly until the click disappears), hold about 300-400 ms, decay about 1.5 s, sustain 0; a little wavetable bend/warp for character; a second envelope (attack about 0.5 ms, hold 0, decay about 35 ms, sustain 0, release about 15 ms) routed to master tune for a kick-like pitch punch (set amount to the middle); tube distortion with mix pulled back; EQ lift around 100 Hz. If the kick already supplies the punch, raise the 808 attack to about 80-100 ms so it fills in behind the kick instead of sidechaining. Pluck/house bass: mono voice, square wave through a 24 dB low-pass, short attack, no hold, low sustain, some release; the same envelope also drives cutoff for the pluck; drive the filter a little; add a second osc (PWM-type wave, 5-7 unison voices, detuned) for a wide top, keep content below about 100-150 Hz mono, reverb and delay with low cut. Growl bass: saw with a low-pass, second osc with 7-8 unison voices, mono, very short release, an LFO shaped as a sidechain duck on both osc levels, cutoff on a macro, then boost about 140 Hz to keep it a bass. Sub: sine, one voice, mono/legato, attack about 5 ms and a short release to avoid clicks.",
 ["A synth with envelopes, mod matrix (envelope → pitch), unison, mono mode, LFO shapes","EQ and distortion","A drum loop with a kick for context"],
 [("bass_type","enum","808 | pluck | growl | sub",True),("kick","audio","The kick it must sit with",False),("key","string","Song key",False)],
 [("bass_patch","synth-patch","Bass patch of the chosen type"),("fit_notes","text","How the bass was fitted around the kick (attack delay, LFO duck or sidechain)")],
 ["Vendor wavetable names (e.g. a named PWM wave) are replaced by generic shapes.",
  "Keep below about 100-150 Hz mono; put width only on higher layers.",
  "Reverb on bass: cut its lows (ideally on a separate return).",
  "For Matt's cinematic/ambient tracks the 808 and sub recipes and the attack-delay trick are the most relevant; the growl is optional for driving sections.",A],
 [("808 body: sine osc, attack near 0 then raised just enough to remove the click, hold about 300-400 ms, decay about 1.5 s, sustain 0; add a slight bend/warp to the wave for character.","Synth amp envelope","A clean sub tone that holds then decays."),
  ("808 punch: second envelope (attack about 0.5 ms, hold 0, decay about 35 ms, sustain 0, release about 15 ms) → master/global tune; set the amount between too little and too much.","Mod matrix","A kick-like pitch blip at the start of each note."),
  ("808 FX: tube distortion with mix pulled back, EQ lift around 100 Hz. Play with the drums: if both kick and 808 punch, raise the 808 attack to about 80-100 ms so the kick provides the transient.","Distortion, EQ","The 808 fills behind the kick without clashing."),
  ("Pluck bass: mono voicing, square osc into a 24 dB low-pass; amp envelope with tiny attack, no hold, low sustain, a bit of release; route the same envelope to cutoff and add a little filter drive.","Synth","A plucky, punchy bass note."),
  ("Pluck top layer: second osc with a PWM-style wave, 5-7 unison voices, detune set for width, through the same filter at lower level; add reverb and delay with their lows cut; keep lows mono below about 100-150 Hz.","Synth + FX","Wide upper texture over a solid mono low end."),
  ("Growl bass: saw + low-pass, second saw osc with 7-8 unison voices, mono, very short release; draw an LFO shaped like a sidechain duck and route it to both osc levels; put cutoff (and optionally reverb/delay mix) on macros; boost around 140 Hz.","Synth + LFO + EQ","A rhythmic ducking growl that sits around the kick."),
  ("Sub: sine, 1 voice, mono/legato, attack about 5 ms, short-but-not-instant release; play low.","Synth","Smooth sub with no clicks at note start/end; overlapping notes glide.")],
 ["808 shows a pitch drop over the first ~35 ms and decays over about 1.5 s.","With the kick, the 808 (with delayed attack) does not double the transient.","Low end below about 120 Hz is mono for all four types.","Sub notes start and stop without audible clicks."],
 ["bass","808","sub-bass","pluck-bass","punch","pitch-envelope","mono-low-end","sidechain"],A)

mk("r45X4PYH-fg","How to Create a Shimmering Pad",
 "self-sustaining-shimmer-pad-few-notes-into-99pct-feedback-asymmetric-stereo-delay-1s-left-150ms-right-melody-dissolves-into-texture-optional-tape-wow",
 "Turn a few played notes into a self-sustaining shimmering pad by feeding them once into a clean stereo delay at 99% feedback with very different left/right times (about 1 s and 150 ms), so the melody dissolves into a continuous texture for the rest of the track",
 "Feedback-delay pad generator. Play a short phrase of three or four notes once into a neutral (uncoloured) stereo delay, 100% of the time in the track afterwards nothing else is fed in. Set feedback to the maximum safe value (99%) and set the delay times far apart: about 1 s on the left and about 150 ms on the right. The repeats keep cycling through the whole track; the original melody vanishes and is replaced by a shimmering secondary texture. Optional: use a tape-style delay instead for pitch warble, which strengthens the shimmer. Use the plain feedback control rather than cross-feed, which grows the signal quickly.",
 ["A stereo delay with independent left/right times and feedback up to 99%","A short note phrase (3-4 notes)"],
 [("phrase","midi","3-4 notes played once at the start",True),("left_delay_s","number","About 1 s",False),("right_delay_s","number","About 0.15 s",False)],
 [("shimmer_pad","audio","Self-sustaining texture that runs under the track")],
 ["Feedback near 100% can run away; feed the source only once and keep cross-feed at zero; put a limiter after the delay as a safety.",
  "Matt prefers steady sounds: the tape-wow option adds pitch wobble, so keep it subtle or skip it.",
  "Web Audio: two DelayNodes (1.0 s, 0.15 s) with GainNode feedback 0.99 and a ChannelMerger reproduce this.",A],
 [("Write a 3-4 note phrase and route it to a stereo delay set 100% wet on that channel (or on a send).","DAW / delay","Only the source phrase feeds the delay."),
  ("Set left delay about 1 s and right delay about 150 ms; set feedback to 99% on both sides; leave cross-feed at zero.","Stereo delay","Repeats sustain indefinitely, with very different rhythms per side."),
  ("Play the phrase once and let it run; do not feed more notes in.","Transport","The melody disappears into a shimmering continuous texture within a few cycles."),
  ("Optional: swap to a tape-style delay with light wow/flutter to exaggerate shimmer.","Tape delay","More perceived shimmer through slight pitch variation."),
  ("Add a limiter or level automation after the delay and check the level over the full track length.","Limiter","Texture stays at a stable level without runaway build-up.")],
 ["Left and right delay times differ by roughly 6-7x (1 s vs 0.15 s).","After the source phrase ends, the texture continues for the length of the track without new input.","Output level stays bounded (no runaway)."],
 ["pad","shimmer","delay","feedback","texture","stereo-width","generative","web-audio"],A)
